    "q-103494": {
      "dom_id": "step-q103494",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Given that $22≡13\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$, which of the following could be the value of $n?$\n\nI. $n=2$\n\nII. $n=3$\n\nIII. $n=4$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWe have $a-b=22-13=9.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Note that $2∤9.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=2.$\n\n- Note that $3∣9$ because $9÷3=3,$ and there is no remainder. Therefore, the statement is true when $n=3.$\n\n- Note that $4∤9.$ Therefore, the statement is false when $n=4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
