    "q-113679": {
      "dom_id": "step-q113679",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $11≡-15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$\n\nII. $-26≡7\\,(\\text{mod}\\,11)$\n\nIII. $-26≡-9\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false because $4∤(11-(-15)).$ Notice that $11-(-15)=26,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is true because $11\\,|\\,(-26-7).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(-26-7)÷11=-33÷11=-3\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false because $5∤(-26-(-9)).$ Notice that $-26-(-9)=-17,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
